That second check was added specifically because testing the flagged trade-off empirically (not left as a documented assumption) showed the naive design was wrong: graphlite resolve run --prefer-file against a real fixture with two same-named functions in different modules of one file returned candidates="2" but still picked a selected_id with no error -- and because graphlite''s stable_id does not encode module nesting, both candidates carried the identical stable_id, so the second-stage graph sym:<id> lookup could not catch the collision either (it resolved to whichever one graphlite internally picked, nodes="1", no ambiguity signal). Checking candidates == 1 closes this gap without reintroducing the original false-positive: the caller-substring case this record exists to fix reports candidates="1" under resolve (already excluded by resolve''s own ranking), while genuine in-file ambiguity reports candidates="2"+ and is now correctly rejected as Ambiguous.

Verified with two real-graphlite integration tests (both #[ignore]-gated, requiring graphlite on PATH, run explicitly): resolves_symbol_with_real_graphlite (unchanged, still passes) and a new real_graphlite_in_file_ambiguity_is_caught_by_the_candidates_check, built against the exact two-same-named-functions fixture used to measure the trade-off, asserting Err(LocatorError::Ambiguous) -- proving the protection holds against real graphlite, not just fake-locator fixtures. Five fake-locator unit tests also updated/added: the two pre-existing tests'' fixtures updated from the symbols/graph protocol to resolve/graph; three new tests cover the wrong-file rejection, the candidates>1 Ambiguous path, and the ''no symbol matched'' non-zero-exit case (confirmed directly that real  exits non-zero with an error message rather than empty XML when nothing matches at all, which run_resolve now maps to Missing rather than letting it fall through to the generic Unavailable classification).

Real-world verification against the exact findings that motivated this record: re-ran strata code-ref-check against this repository with the fix installed. All three of EDR-0017''s SYMBOL-AMBIGUOUS false positives (backfill_code_reference_ids, Store::update_code_reference, Store::remove_code_reference) now resolve correctly; each moved to LINE-DRIFT (a real, separate, expected finding -- those three references had no line range ever recorded) and were closed with strata code-ref-update using the now-resolvable locations. EDR-0017''s fourth SYMBOL-AMBIGUOUS finding (cli/src/args.rs, query ''Command'') remains, correctly: ''Command'' is a real, common name genuinely colliding across multiple distinct real symbols in this codebase, not a false positive, and it already has a working line-range fallback reference.

Full workspace test suite: clean, zero failures, including cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs''s own fake-locator fixture (updated to the resolve protocol). cargo build/test/fmt/clippy all clean in both debug and release profiles.', NULL);

UNEXPECTED HEADLINE FINDING, independently reproduced: the currently-shipped PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT (''pandoc {input} -o {output} --standalone ...'', cli/src/config.rs) has three live, silent content-corruption defects, verified directly against real records:
(1) Pandoc''s ''smart'' extension (on by default in the markdown reader) converts literal ''--'' to an en-dash (U+2013) in prose context -- confirmed present in real rendered output. Since Strata''s CLI flags are written as literal ''--check'', ''--patch'', etc., these can render incorrectly in the published HTML.
(2) Pandoc''s ''raw_html'' extension (on by default) treats angle-bracket placeholder text like ''<id>'' or ''<kind>'' as a raw (unknown) HTML tag rather than literal text, emitting it unescaped into the HTML instead of as ''&lt;id&gt;''. Confirmed directly: ADR-0009''s ''strata graph <id> remains the way to discover...'' renders with a raw, unescaped ''<id>'' in the HTML source, which a browser will not display as visible text since it is interpreted as an unrecognized tag rather than escaped characters.
(3) Pandoc''s ''raw_tex'' extension (on by default) silently deletes text it interprets as LaTeX. Confirmed directly and dramatically: PDR-0004''s own validation regex ''^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-\d{3}$'' renders in the published HTML as ''^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-$'' -- the ''\d{3}'' fragment is silently deleted, changing the documented regex''s actual meaning with no error or warning.

Verified fix: adding ''-f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex'' to PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT (disabling exactly those three extensions, everything else including native_spans/bracketed_spans/header_attributes stays on). Re-verified independently: all three defects resolved (regex fragment preserved, ''<id>'' correctly escaped to ''&lt;id&gt;'', no en-dash present), full pipeline (real template, metadata, CSS, xref.lua filter) still exits 0.

SECOND CONFIRMED BUG, directly relevant to PDR-0004: cli/assets/site/xref.lua''s record-ID matching pattern is hardcoded to exactly three letters (''[A-Z][A-Z][A-Z]%-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]'' in Lua pattern syntax) -- confirmed by direct inspection. PDR-0004 Section 5.8 states this file ''contains no hardcoded record-kind pattern (verified), so it needs no change'' -- that statement is incorrect. All five of PDR-0004''s proposed kind codes (SPEC, ASMT, RSCH, GLOS, RISK) are four letters and would never be recognized or linked by this filter as currently written. Fix (untested by this evidence entry, proposed by the research pass): change the pattern to ''[A-Z][A-Z][A-Z][A-Z]?%-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]''.

OTHER FINDINGS:
- Glossary term-linking: no special Pandoc feature is needed or worth adopting. An ordinary link Strata already has the means to construct (via xref.lua''s existing site_index-driven rewriting of bare record-ID mentions) fully solves this; Pandoc''s Span/attribute extensions (bracketed_spans, link_attributes, native_spans) are all on by default and available if a future styling hook is wanted, at zero configuration cost.
- pandoc-crossref (a separate, Pandoc-version-coupled filter binary for LaTeX-style auto-numbered cross-references): not a good fit. Strata''s identifiers are already stable and human-meaningful; auto-numbering would be actively harmful, it operates only within one Pandoc invocation (publish renders one record per invocation, so it cannot do cross-document referencing anyway), and it reintroduces exactly the version-coupling failure mode already found for Pandoc''s own JSON API gate (RFC-0006-EV-004), as a second binary.
- Pandoc''s built-in --citeproc/bibliography/citation machinery: works correctly and is well-documented, but is a mismatch for Strata''s internal record-to-record references. It renders citations as ''(Strata 2026, sec. 3)'' style text (wrong -- Strata wants ''ADR-0019''), its link-citations option links to an in-page References block rather than the target record''s own page (two hops instead of one), and it would require generating a second full graph projection in an external bibliographic JSON schema. Verdict: do not adopt for internal references; potentially revisit narrowly for a future Research kind''s external-source citations only.
- Table of contents / heading IDs: Pandoc''s default auto_identifiers scheme is title-derived and confirmed unstable under both retitling and unrelated insertions elsewhere in the same document (a heading''s positional de-duplication suffix, e.g. ''definition-1'', shifts when an earlier heading is added) -- the same anchor-instability class ADR-0012/EDR-0003 already solved at the file-slug level, now reproduced at the heading level. Fix, confirmed: Pandoc''s header_attributes extension (on by default) honors an explicit ''{#custom-id}'' on any heading exactly as given, including leading digits, with no stripping or further transformation. This directly enables PDR-0004''s proposed anchor scheme, but only if the renderer is changed to emit an explicit ''{#{number:04}-{slug}}'' attribute on every generated heading (today''s Block::Heading has no attribute/id field). --id-prefix should never be used; it prefixes explicit IDs too, breaking any href computed from them. --toc/--toc-depth works and respects explicit IDs, but Strata''s shipped template.html has no $ placeholder, so it currently renders nothing even if passed.
- pandoc -f json -t html direct entry point: confirmed to work end-to-end (49/49 of Strata''s real records produce byte-identical HTML via the JSON route versus the current Markdown-text route). However, recommendation is to NOT adopt this as the publish pipeline: ''single pass, no intermediate representation'' is not the right framing, since strata export already requires a deterministic Markdown-text renderer regardless (ADR-0003/ADR-0010) and cannot be removed; going AST-direct for publish specifically would mean maintaining two renderers from one document model (with no mechanical check that they stay consistent) instead of today''s one renderer reused for both purposes, would require a nontrivial comrak-AST-to-Pandoc-AST mapping layer with its own silent-bug surface (confirmed one directly: an accidentally-set RowHeadColumns value silently converted body table cells to header cells), and would hard-couple publish''s renderer contract to Pandoc-AST-JSON specifically, contradicting ADR-0019''s explicit design goal that swapping the static-site generator requires only a configuration change. Recommendation: keep the Markdown-text pipeline, fix the three live reader-extension bugs above instead; keep the JSON-AST route in reserve as a targeted escape hatch only if a future requirement genuinely cannot be expressed in Markdown text.', NULL);

Of those, at least 7 distinct exhaustive match constructs over RecordKind''s four current variants live in records/src/kind.rs alone: the ALL const array, and the code/purpose/slug/initial_status/statuses functions (two separate array literals within the statuses-related functions). records/src/validation.rs has its own exhaustive table (required-fields per kind, used by strata schema) plus a golden test. cli/src/document.rs has the EDR-0004 JSON-to-Markdown section-mapping table, one exhaustive match per kind. store/src/restore.rs has two sites, both round-trip/demo test data rather than production logic. cli/src/export.rs, cli/src/render.rs, and cli/src/render_backend.rs also carry kind-keyed string tables (not yet confirmed exhaustive-match vs. lookup-table shape) that would need the same audit pass.

This is bounded, not sprawling -- a handful of core files (records/src/kind.rs and records/src/validation.rs) carry the real exhaustive-match burden, and most of the 25-file surface is call sites that would simply need to compile against a larger enum, not files requiring new per-kind logic. This is useful evidence for the follow-on design record''s migration-strategy question (this RFC''s own open Question 6 in RFC-0011, and this RFC''s own outcome): expanding RecordKind from 4 to 9 variants (RFC-0006''s Specification/Assessment/Research plus RFC-0011''s Glossary/Risk) is real, compiler-enforced work at a known, checkable set of sites -- exactly the guarantee rule 2 promises (the compiler fails to build until every match site is updated) -- rather than an open-ended unknown. Not yet assessed: which of these 25 call sites need genuinely new per-kind behavior (a Specification''s hierarchy, a Glossary/Risk''s aggregate export) versus simply gaining a new arm that mirrors an existing one.', NULL);

Three independent, confirmed failures rule out Pandoc as parser or storage format:
(1) Round-trip fidelity: only 33/49 records were AST-stable after one markdown-to-AST-to-markdown round-trip even under Pandoc''s best available flags. Reproduced directly on PDR-0004''s own Requirements section: a single paragraph (Strata''s own backslash-escaped list-like lines) silently becomes Para+BulletList after one round-trip -- confirmed independently with the exact command used, block sequence verified before (Para) and after (Header, Para, BulletList, Header). Pandoc''s default markdown reader also silently rewrites literal ''--'' to an en-dash (U+2013) in record text (e.g. ''export --check'' became ''export –check''), confirmed independently.
(2) YAML frontmatter handling: round-tripping ADR-0019 through Pandoc''s best flags reorders frontmatter keys alphabetically, strips quoting from scalar values, and silently drops the empty sequence ''tags: []'' entirely -- all three confirmed independently by diffing real output.
(3) Long-term storage viability: Pandoc hard-rejects any JSON AST whose pandoc-api-version does not exactly match the running binary''s own version, with no migration path. Confirmed independently: bumping a real AST''s api-version field by one point release caused pandoc to refuse to decode it, exit 64, ''Incompatible API versions''. A stored AST would become unreadable by pandoc itself after an unrelated version upgrade on the host machine.

No pure-Rust crate provides a way to produce Pandoc''s own AST format without invoking the pandoc binary (pandoc_types and pandoc_ast crates on crates.io are typed wrappers only, requiring pandoc itself to produce the JSON; neither implements Pandoc''s own Markdown dialect).

comrak 0.54 scored 49/49 idempotent and 49/49 full-AST-stable on the identical corpus, preserves YAML frontmatter as a verbatim extracted block (does not parse it -- Strata keeps doing that itself), and provides native source-position spans on every AST node with no reader-dependent distortion, unlike Pandoc where the +sourcepos extension is unsupported by the markdown reader entirely and wraps every block/inline in synthetic Div/Span nodes where it does work. Recommendation: comrak for parsing (CommonMark + GFM tables/strikethrough/autolink/tasklist, frontmatter block extraction only), Pandoc unchanged as the ADR-0019 rendering subprocess, Strata''s own cli/src/render.rs retained as the production renderer to protect ADR-0010''s byte-for-byte guarantee.', NULL);
