Two existing mechanisms already cover the real need without a new entity. First, code_reference (store/migrations/V1__initial.sql) already exists specifically for this: it stores no content at all, only path/symbol/line_start/line_end, and is re-verified live against the working tree by strata code-ref-check (RFC-0007, EDR-0012). A stored, independently-updatable snippet copy is strictly worse than this pointer-based approach -- a copy can drift silently from the code it once matched (exactly the staleness this session''s documentation audit found in EDR-0008: five stale line-number citations nothing had re-verified), where a pointer either still resolves or code-ref-check catches it. Second, EDR-0006 already considered and rejected a global, independently-addressable identity for evidence in general, specifically because ''evidence is only ever looked up scoped to its owning record'' -- the same premise that sinks a joinable snippet table now; no new session has surfaced evidence that premise has stopped holding.

One legitimate idea survives and is worth keeping for whoever next touches publish/render: number a record''s code_reference entries in document order and render each as a captioned, syntax-highlighted figure by reading the cited path/line range live from the working tree at render time -- ''see Figure 1'' with zero schema change and zero staleness risk, since the content is pulled fresh rather than stored. This belongs with PDR-0002''s static publication design work, not as a new storage mechanism.', NULL);




