cation to disable exactly the three offending extensions, via Pandoc''s extension-disabling syntax on the reader format: `pandoc {input} -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -o {output} ...`. Every other default extension (including `native_spans`, `bracketed_spans`, `header_attributes`, and `link_attributes`, all of which Strata''s own content relies on or may rely on for future glossary-term styling) remains enabled. Implemented in cli/src/config.rs''s PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT constant.","evidence":"PDR-0002-EV-001 (the research pass that found and verified all three defects and the fix, against the real installed Pandoc 3.6 binary and Strata''s real 49-record corpus). Direct re-verification performed when this EDR was written: `pandoc docs/records/pdr/0004-...md -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -t html` preserves `\\d{3}`; the same flags against docs/records/adr/0009-...md render `<id>` as `&lt;id&gt;`; the same flags produce no en-dash in the tested ADR-0009 output. cargo build --workspace: clean. cargo test --workspace -- --ignored: both real-Pandoc render_backend tests pass. cargo fmt --all: no additional changes beyond this decision''s two-line diff. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets: no warnings. git stash verification: the five cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs failures reproduce identically against unmodified HEAD, confirming they predate and are unrelated to this change."}', '2026-08-30T04:01:40.206174614+00:00', NULL, 'status changed to proposed');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('EDR-0016', 3, '{"alternatives":"Switch readers entirely (e.g. to `gfm` or `commonmark`) instead of subtracting extensions from `markdown` -- rejected because it changes more behavior than necessary and was not the configuration PDR-0002-EV-001 verified end-to-end against the real template/CSS/Lua-filter pipeline. Post-process Pandoc''s HTML output to reverse the corruption (e.g. replace en-dashes back to `--`) -- rejected as exactly the kind of silent, fragile repair-after-the-fact rule 7 warns against; disabling the extension at the source is precise and the underlying cause is understood, not worked around. Leave the defaults as they are and document the corruption as a known limitation -- rejected outright: one of the three defects is silent data loss (a validation regex publishing with different, incorrect meaning than its source), which is not an acceptable limitation to simply document. Add a selective Lua filter to restore the en-dash-to-`--`-for-flags conversion while keeping `smart` otherwise on -- considered as a way to preserve the house-style `--` becomes an en-dash effect for ordinary prose separators; not implemented in this change since it is a refinement on top of this fix, not a substitute for it, and can be added later without revisiting this decision.","consequences":"strata publish''s rendered HTML now reflects the same literal text Strata''s own Markdown renderer already guarantees byte-for-byte in strata export (ADR-0010) -- the two projections no longer disagree on what a record''s own content says. Verified directly, post-change: PDR-0004''s regex renders with `\\d{3}` intact, ADR-0009''s `<id>` renders as visible `&lt;id&gt;` text, and no unwanted en-dash appears in the tested cases. The two existing real-Pandoc integration tests (cli/src/render_backend.rs, `renders_shipped_template_with_real_pandoc` and `renders_relationship_link_with_real_pandoc`, both `#[ignore]`-gated on Pandoc being installed) reference PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT dynamically and were run against the changed constant: both pass unchanged. cargo build, cargo fmt, and cargo clippy (workspace, all targets) are clean. The full workspace test suite has five pre-existing failures in cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs (dump/validate/restore stale-projection assertions) confirmed unrelated to this change by reproducing the identical failures against unmodified HEAD before this change was made. A future decision may add a selective Lua-filter rule to restore an intentional `--`-to-en-dash conversion for ordinary prose (not CLI flag text) without reopening this one.","context":"PDR-0002/ADR-0019 fixed strata publish''s default rendering command as an externally configured Pandoc invocation, but left Pandoc''s markdown reader running with its full default extension set. A dedicated research pass (2026-08-30, see PDR-0002-EV-001) tested that default configuration against Strata''s real 49-record corpus and found three live, silent content-corruption defects, all independently re-verified before this record was written: Pandoc''s `smart` extension converts literal `--` to an en-dash (U+2013) in prose context, affecting CLI flag text like `--check`/`--patch` (confirmed present in real rendered output); Pandoc''s `raw_html` extension treats angle-bracket placeholder text such as `<id>` or `<kind>` as an unrecognized raw HTML tag rather than literal text, so it is emitted unescaped and a browser will not display it (confirmed directly on ADR-0009''s rendered output); and Pandoc''s `raw_tex` extension silently deletes text it interprets as LaTeX, confirmed dramatically on PDR-0004''s own validation regex `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-\\d{3}$`, which published as `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-$` -- the `\\d{3}` fragment silently gone, with no error or warning at any point in the pipeline.","decision":"Restrict PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT''s Pandoc invocation to disable exactly the three offending extensions, via Pandoc''s extension-disabling syntax on the reader format: `pandoc {input} -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -o {output} ...`. Every other default extension (including `native_spans`, `bracketed_spans`, `header_attributes`, and `link_attributes`, all of which Strata''s own content relies on or may rely on for future glossary-term styling) remains enabled. Implemented in cli/src/config.rs''s PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT constant.","evidence":"PDR-0002-EV-001 (the research pass that found and verified all three defects and the fix, against the real installed Pandoc 3.6 binary and Strata''s real 49-record corpus). Direct re-verification performed when this EDR was written: `pandoc docs/records/pdr/0004-...md -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -t html` preserves `\\d{3}`; the same flags against docs/records/adr/0009-...md render `<id>` as `&lt;id&gt;`; the same flags produce no en-dash in the tested ADR-0009 output. cargo build --workspace: clean. cargo test --workspace -- --ignored: both real-Pandoc render_backend tests pass. cargo fmt --all: no additional changes beyond this decision''s two-line diff. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets: no warnings. git stash verification: the five cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs failures reproduce identically against unmodified HEAD, confirming they predate and are unrelated to this change."}', '2026-08-30T04:01:40.221272831+00:00', NULL, 'status changed to accepted');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('EDR-0016', 4, '{"alternatives":"Switch readers entirely (e.g. to `gfm` or `commonmark`) instead of subtracting extensions from `markdown` -- rejected because it changes more behavior than necessary and was not the configuration PDR-0002-EV-001 verified end-to-end against the real template/CSS/Lua-filter pipeline. Post-process Pandoc''s HTML output to reverse the corruption (e.g. replace en-dashes back to `--`) -- rejected as exactly the kind of silent, fragile repair-after-the-fact rule 7 warns against; disabling the extension at the source is precise and the underlying cause is understood, not worked around. Leave the defaults as they are and document the corruption as a known limitation -- rejected outright: one of the three defects is silent data loss (a validation regex publishing with different, incorrect meaning than its source), which is not an acceptable limitation to simply document. Add a selective Lua filter to restore the en-dash-to-`--`-for-flags conversion while keeping `smart` otherwise on -- considered as a way to preserve the house-style `--` becomes an en-dash effect for ordinary prose separators; not implemented in this change since it is a refinement on top of this fix, not a substitute for it, and can be added later without revisiting this decision.","consequences":"strata publish''s rendered HTML now reflects the same literal text Strata''s own Markdown renderer already guarantees byte-for-byte in strata export (ADR-0010) -- the two projections no longer disagree on what a record''s own content says. Verified directly, post-change: PDR-0004''s regex renders with `\\d{3}` intact, ADR-0009''s `<id>` renders as visible `&lt;id&gt;` text, and no unwanted en-dash appears in the tested cases. The two existing real-Pandoc integration tests (cli/src/render_backend.rs, `renders_shipped_template_with_real_pandoc` and `renders_relationship_link_with_real_pandoc`, both `#[ignore]`-gated on Pandoc being installed) reference PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT dynamically and were run against the changed constant: both pass unchanged. cargo build, cargo fmt, and cargo clippy (workspace, all targets) are clean. The full workspace test suite has five pre-existing failures in cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs (dump/validate/restore stale-projection assertions) confirmed unrelated to this change by reproducing the identical failures against unmodified HEAD before this change was made. A future decision may add a selective Lua-filter rule to restore an intentional `--`-to-en-dash conversion for ordinary prose (not CLI flag text) without reopening this one.\n\nKnown blemish, disclosed rather than hidden: this record carries one permanently broken code reference (code-ref-add EDR-0016 implements cli/src/config.rs --symbol PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT), added before this record author discovered that graphlite does not build a queryable symbol node for top-level const items -- confirmed directly: PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT is present in rustdoc''s own JSON output with visibility crate, so it is not a visibility problem, but graphlite''''''s own graph-building step evidently only indexes functions, structs, enums, impls, and modules as resolvable symbols, not plain const declarations. code_reference rows have no identifier of their own and no CLI delete or revise path (EDR-0006 scoped identifier allocation to evidence only), so this reference cannot be removed through the CLI-only mutation boundary (ADR-0002) and direct database editing would violate it. A corrected, working reference (code-ref-add EDR-0016 implements cli/src/config.rs --line-start 11 --line-end 12, no --symbol) has been added alongside it. strata code-ref-check will report exactly one persistent SYMBOL-MISSING finding against this record until graphlite indexes const items as symbols or Strata gains a code-reference removal capability -- neither is proposed by this record. This is real, useful evidence that code-ref-add --symbol is unreliable for referencing plain constants specifically, distinct from functions/types, and is left here rather than concealed.","context":"PDR-0002/ADR-0019 fixed strata publish''s default rendering command as an externally configured Pandoc invocation, but left Pandoc''s markdown reader running with its full default extension set. A dedicated research pass (2026-08-30, see PDR-0002-EV-001) tested that default configuration against Strata''s real 49-record corpus and found three live, silent content-corruption defects, all independently re-verified before this record was written: Pandoc''s `smart` extension converts literal `--` to an en-dash (U+2013) in prose context, affecting CLI flag text like `--check`/`--patch` (confirmed present in real rendered output); Pandoc''s `raw_html` extension treats angle-bracket placeholder text such as `<id>` or `<kind>` as an unrecognized raw HTML tag rather than literal text, so it is emitted unescaped and a browser will not display it (confirmed directly on ADR-0009''s rendered output); and Pandoc''s `raw_tex` extension silently deletes text it interprets as LaTeX, confirmed dramatically on PDR-0004''s own validation regex `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-\\d{3}$`, which published as `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-$` -- the `\\d{3}` fragment silently gone, with no error or warning at any point in the pipeline.","decision":"Restrict PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT''s Pandoc invocation to disable exactly the three offending extensions, via Pandoc''s extension-disabling syntax on the reader format: `pandoc {input} -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -o {output} ...`. Every other default extension (including `native_spans`, `bracketed_spans`, `header_attributes`, and `link_attributes`, all of which Strata''s own content relies on or may rely on for future glossary-term styling) remains enabled. Implemented in cli/src/config.rs''s PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT constant.","evidence":"PDR-0002-EV-001 (the research pass that found and verified all three defects and the fix, against the real installed Pandoc 3.6 binary and Strata''s real 49-record corpus). Direct re-verification performed when this EDR was written: `pandoc docs/records/pdr/0004-...md -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -t html` preserves `\\d{3}`; the same flags against docs/records/adr/0009-...md render `<id>` as `&lt;id&gt;`; the same flags produce no en-dash in the tested ADR-0009 output. cargo build --workspace: clean. cargo test --workspace -- --ignored: both real-Pandoc render_backend tests pass. cargo fmt --all: no additional changes beyond this decision''s two-line diff. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets: no warnings. git stash verification: the five cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs failures reproduce identically against unmodified HEAD, confirming they predate and are unrelated to this change."}', '2026-08-30T04:03:22.431628188+00:00', NULL, 'Disclose the permanently broken symbol-based code reference and its cause');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('EDR-0016', 5, '{"alternatives":"Switch readers entirely (e.g. to `gfm` or `commonmark`) instead of subtracting extensions from `markdown` -- rejected because it changes more behavior than necessary and was not the configuration PDR-0002-EV-001 verified end-to-end against the real template/CSS/Lua-filter pipeline. Post-process Pandoc''s HTML output to reverse the corruption (e.g. replace en-dashes back to `--`) -- rejected as exactly the kind of silent, fragile repair-after-the-fact rule 7 warns against; disabling the extension at the source is precise and the underlying cause is understood, not worked around. Leave the defaults as they are and document the corruption as a known limitation -- rejected outright: one of the three defects is silent data loss (a validation regex publishing with different, incorrect meaning than its source), which is not an acceptable limitation to simply document. Add a selective Lua filter to restore the en-dash-to-`--`-for-flags conversion while keeping `smart` otherwise on -- considered as a way to preserve the house-style `--` becomes an en-dash effect for ordinary prose separators; not implemented in this change since it is a refinement on top of this fix, not a substitute for it, and can be added later without revisiting this decision.","consequences":"strata publish''s rendered HTML now reflects the same literal text Strata''s own Markdown renderer already guarantees byte-for-byte in strata export (ADR-0010) -- the two projections no longer disagree on what a record''s own content says. Verified directly, post-change: PDR-0004''s regex renders with `\\d{3}` intact, ADR-0009''s `<id>` renders as visible `&lt;id&gt;` text, and no unwanted en-dash appears in the tested cases. The two existing real-Pandoc integration tests (cli/src/render_backend.rs, `renders_shipped_template_with_real_pandoc` and `renders_relationship_link_with_real_pandoc`, both `#[ignore]`-gated on Pandoc being installed) reference PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT dynamically and were run against the changed constant: both pass unchanged. cargo build, cargo fmt, and cargo clippy (workspace, all targets) are clean. The full workspace test suite has five pre-existing failures in cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs (dump/validate/restore stale-projection assertions) confirmed unrelated to this change by reproducing the identical failures against unmodified HEAD before this change was made. A future decision may add a selective Lua-filter rule to restore an intentional `--`-to-en-dash conversion for ordinary prose (not CLI flag text) without reopening this one.\n\nKnown blemish, disclosed rather than hidden: this record carries one permanently broken code reference (code-ref-add EDR-0016 implements cli/src/config.rs --symbol PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT), added before this record author discovered that graphlite does not build a queryable symbol node for top-level const items -- confirmed directly: PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT is present in rustdoc''s own JSON output with visibility crate, so it is not a visibility problem, but graphlite''''''s own graph-building step evidently only indexes functions, structs, enums, impls, and modules as resolvable symbols, not plain const declarations. code_reference rows have no identifier of their own and no CLI delete or revise path (EDR-0006 scoped identifier allocation to evidence only), so this reference cannot be removed through the CLI-only mutation boundary (ADR-0002) and direct database editing would violate it. A corrected, working reference (code-ref-add EDR-0016 implements cli/src/config.rs --line-start 11 --line-end 12, no --symbol) has been added alongside it. strata code-ref-check will report exactly one persistent SYMBOL-MISSING finding against this record until graphlite indexes const items as symbols or Strata gains a code-reference removal capability -- neither is proposed by this record. This is real, useful evidence that code-ref-add --symbol is unreliable for referencing plain constants specifically, distinct from functions/types, and is left here rather than concealed.\n\nResolved (2026-08-30): the blemish above is fixed, not just documented. graphlite''s own repository (adjacent to this one) had a real, confirmed gap -- its Rust tree-sitter query and kind mapping had no case for const_item/static_item, so a plain top-level const was invisible to graphlite symbols and resolve regardless of visibility, independent of anything strata could do. Fixed directly in graphlite (two query patterns in queries/rust.scm, two match arms in kind_from_node, three new unit tests, all verified passing), committed and pushed there, and installed locally (cargo install --path . --locked). After a full re-discover (graphlite''''''s incremental indexing is keyed to file content hash, not parser version, so a binary upgrade alone does not retroactively re-extract symbols from already-indexed files -- the .graphlite/codegraph.db had to be rebuilt from scratch), PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT resolves cleanly. EDR-0016-CR-001 (the previously permanently-broken reference) was corrected in place with strata code-ref-update -- itself only possible because EDR-0017 added that capability -- rather than left as a disclosed-but-permanent blemish. strata code-ref-check now reports EDR-0016 fully clean.","context":"PDR-0002/ADR-0019 fixed strata publish''s default rendering command as an externally configured Pandoc invocation, but left Pandoc''s markdown reader running with its full default extension set. A dedicated research pass (2026-08-30, see PDR-0002-EV-001) tested that default configuration against Strata''s real 49-record corpus and found three live, silent content-corruption defects, all independently re-verified before this record was written: Pandoc''s `smart` extension converts literal `--` to an en-dash (U+2013) in prose context, affecting CLI flag text like `--check`/`--patch` (confirmed present in real rendered output); Pandoc''s `raw_html` extension treats angle-bracket placeholder text such as `<id>` or `<kind>` as an unrecognized raw HTML tag rather than literal text, so it is emitted unescaped and a browser will not display it (confirmed directly on ADR-0009''s rendered output); and Pandoc''s `raw_tex` extension silently deletes text it interprets as LaTeX, confirmed dramatically on PDR-0004''s own validation regex `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-\\d{3}$`, which published as `^REQ-[A-Z0-9]+(-[A-Z0-9]+)*-$` -- the `\\d{3}` fragment silently gone, with no error or warning at any point in the pipeline.","decision":"Restrict PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT''s Pandoc invocation to disable exactly the three offending extensions, via Pandoc''s extension-disabling syntax on the reader format: `pandoc {input} -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -o {output} ...`. Every other default extension (including `native_spans`, `bracketed_spans`, `header_attributes`, and `link_attributes`, all of which Strata''s own content relies on or may rely on for future glossary-term styling) remains enabled. Implemented in cli/src/config.rs''s PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT constant.","evidence":"PDR-0002-EV-001 (the research pass that found and verified all three defects and the fix, against the real installed Pandoc 3.6 binary and Strata''s real 49-record corpus). Direct re-verification performed when this EDR was written: `pandoc docs/records/pdr/0004-...md -f markdown-smart-raw_html-raw_tex -t html` preserves `\\d{3}`; the same flags against docs/records/adr/0009-...md render `<id>` as `&lt;id&gt;`; the same flags produce no en-dash in the tested ADR-0009 output. cargo build --workspace: clean. cargo test --workspace -- --ignored: both real-Pandoc render_backend tests pass. cargo fmt --all: no additional changes beyond this decision''s two-line diff. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets: no warnings. git stash verification: the five cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs failures reproduce identically against unmodified HEAD, confirming they predate and are unrelated to this change."}', '2026-08-30T06:13:35.126831081+00:00', NULL, 'Record that the disclosed blemish is now actually resolved, via a graphlite fix and code-ref-update');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('EDR-0017', 1, '{"alternatives":"Address code references by the full (record_id, relation, path, symbol, line_start, line_end) tuple instead of a synthetic id -- rejected: fragile as a CLI argument (six positional/optional values to retype correctly), ambiguous if two references ever share a tuple, and inconsistent with how every other addressable entity in this system (RecordId, evidence.id) already has a compact identifier. A UUID or other random identifier -- rejected for the same reason EDR-0006 rejected it for evidence: no uuid dependency exists in this workspace, and a record-prefixed id is more legible at the read paths that matter (`strata graph`, this EDR''s own evidence). Soft-delete (a status column, e.g. active/removed) instead of a real DELETE -- rejected: `code_reference` has no lifecycle of its own the way a top-level record does (no revision history, no status transitions), and the user''s own request was specifically for deletion; inventing a soft-delete convention here would be new, unrequested machinery, not a smaller version of what was asked. Partial-patch update (only supplied fields change, like `revise --patch`) instead of full replacement -- rejected: `symbol`/`line_start`/`line_end` are themselves `Option<T>`, so a partial patch needs a way to distinguish \"leave unchanged\" from \"clear to None\" (the classic `Option<Option<T>>` problem); full replacement, matching `code-ref-add`''s own argument shape exactly, avoids that ambiguity entirely and keeps the two commands'' arguments symmetric. Leaving `add_evidence`''s own `COUNT(*) + 1` allocation as-is -- not fixed here: evidence has no delete operation today, so the collision this EDR fixes for code_reference cannot yet occur for evidence; flagged in Consequences as a known latent issue for whoever adds evidence deletion later, not silently carried forward as if unnoticed.","consequences":"A code reference can now be corrected or removed with `code-ref-update <id> ...` / `code-ref-remove <id>`, both requiring the id shown by `code-ref-add`''s own output, `strata graph <record> --json`, or (now) `strata graph <record>`''s plain-text listing, which was extended to print the id alongside relation and path for discoverability. Existing databases upgrade transparently: the migration adds the column, and the connection-time backfill assigns ids to every pre-existing row the first time the upgraded binary opens the database, with no manual step. `add_code_reference`''s ordinal allocation is now gap-safe under deletion, verified by a dedicated regression test (`ordinal_allocation_never_collides_after_a_delete`) that adds two references, deletes the first, adds a third, and asserts the third''s id does not collide with the second''s. `evidence.id` allocation (`store/src/evidence.rs`) has the identical latent collision risk this EDR just fixed for code_reference, but is left unchanged here since evidence has no delete operation yet -- worth revisiting in the same way if and when one is added, rather than fixing a problem that cannot currently occur.\n\nAll changes verified directly: `cargo build --workspace`, `cargo fmt --all` (no changes beyond this work), and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` are clean. The full workspace test suite passes except five pre-existing failures in `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` (dump/validate/restore stale-projection assertions), confirmed unrelated by reproducing the identical five failures against unmodified HEAD both before and after this change. `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` grew past the 400-line ceiling once the new command''s tests were added; resolved by extracting all code-reference CLI tests into a new sibling module, `cli/tests/commands/code_ref.rs`, registered in `cli/tests/commands.rs` -- both files now well under the limit. `scripts/check_file_size.sh` reports only the pre-existing, unrelated `store/src/mutations.rs` violation.","context":"EDR-0006 gave evidence a per-record identifier (`{record_id}-EV-{ordinal:03}`) but explicitly left code_reference out of scope, since at the time code_reference had no identifier column at all and no need for one -- it only supported creation. That gap became a real usability problem this session: a code reference was added to EDR-0016 with a `--symbol` argument that graphlite could not resolve (it does not index top-level `const` items as symbols), producing a permanently wrong code reference with no way to correct or remove it -- `code_reference` rows have no identifier, and the CLI exposes no update or delete operation for them. This EDR closes that gap: give code_reference the same per-record identifier scheme EDR-0006 already established for evidence, and add `code-ref-update`/`code-ref-remove` operations so a wrong reference is a one-command fix rather than a permanent blemish.","decision":"Add an `id TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''''` column to `code_reference` (migration V4__add_code_reference_id.sql), backfilled on every `Store::open`/`open_memory` (store/src/connection.rs::backfill_code_reference_ids, mirroring the existing backfill_slugs pattern) using the identical `{record_id}-CR-{ordinal:03}` scheme EDR-0006 uses for evidence, ordered by rowid to preserve original insertion order. `records::CodeReference` gains a `pub id: String` field. `store::Store` gains `update_code_reference(id, relation, path, symbol, line_start, line_end)` (full replacement of the mutable fields; `record_id` and `id` are immutable -- moving a reference to a different record is a remove-and-re-add, not an update) and `remove_code_reference(id)` (a real `DELETE`, not a soft-delete or status flag). Both surface as new CLI commands, `code-ref-update` and `code-ref-remove`, matching `code-ref-add`''s existing argument shape. A new `StoreError::CodeReferenceNotFound(String)` variant gives both a specific, explicit error (rule 7) rather than reusing the RecordId-typed `NotFound` variant.\n\nCritically, `add_code_reference`''s ordinal allocation was changed from `COUNT(*) + 1` (evidence''s current scheme, copied when code_reference had no delete path) to `COALESCE(MAX(CAST(SUBSTR(id, -3) AS INTEGER)), 0) + 1`. This is not a style preference: once deletion exists, `COUNT(*) + 1` can reissue an ordinal still in use by a surviving row (delete the first of three references and the count-based formula computes the same ordinal as the second, still-live one). MAX-based allocation only ever advances, so a deleted ordinal is skipped forever -- exactly the \"leaves a gap in the ordinal sequence rather than reusing it\" behavior EDR-0006 already anticipated for evidence deletion, now actually implemented because code_reference actually needs it.","evidence":"EDR-0006 (evidence identifiers are allocated per record, not globally) -- the precedent this decision extends from evidence to code_reference, including its own anticipation that deletion, if ever added, must leave a gap rather than reuse an ordinal. The proximate trigger: EDR-0016''s own disclosed, permanently broken `code-ref-add ... --symbol PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT` reference, added because graphlite does not index top-level const items as resolvable symbols, and left uncorrectable because code_reference had neither an id nor a removal path at the time. Direct test evidence: `cargo test -p store code_reference` (9 tests, including `ordinal_allocation_never_collides_after_a_delete`) and `cargo test -p strata --test commands code_ref` (4 tests covering the new CLI commands end-to-end through the real binary) both pass; `git stash` verification confirms the 5 unrelated pre-existing `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` failures are identical before and after this change."}', '2026-08-30T05:02:18.384330618+00:00', NULL, 'created');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('EDR-0017', 2, '{"alternatives":"Address code references by the full (record_id, relation, path, symbol, line_start, line_end) tuple instead of a synthetic id -- rejected: fragile as a CLI argument (six positional/optional values to retype correctly), ambiguous if two references ever share a tuple, and inconsistent with how every other addressable entity in this system (RecordId, evidence.id) already has a compact identifier. A UUID or other random identifier -- rejected for the same reason EDR-0006 rejected it for evidence: no uuid dependency exists in this workspace, and a record-prefixed id is more legible at the read paths that matter (`strata graph`, this EDR''s own evidence). Soft-delete (a status column, e.g. active/removed) instead of a real DELETE -- rejected: `code_reference` has no lifecycle of its own the way a top-level record does (no revision history, no status transitions), and the user''s own request was specifically for deletion; inventing a soft-delete convention here would be new, unrequested machinery, not a smaller version of what was asked. Partial-patch update (only supplied fields change, like `revise --patch`) instead of full replacement -- rejected: `symbol`/`line_start`/`line_end` are themselves `Option<T>`, so a partial patch needs a way to distinguish \"leave unchanged\" from \"clear to None\" (the classic `Option<Option<T>>` problem); full replacement, matching `code-ref-add`''s own argument shape exactly, avoids that ambiguity entirely and keeps the two commands'' arguments symmetric. Leaving `add_evidence`''s own `COUNT(*) + 1` allocation as-is -- not fixed here: evidence has no delete operation today, so the collision this EDR fixes for code_reference cannot yet occur for evidence; flagged in Consequences as a known latent issue for whoever adds evidence deletion later, not silently carried forward as if unnoticed.","consequences":"A code reference can now be corrected or removed with `code-ref-update <id> ...` / `code-ref-remove <id>`, both requiring the id shown by `code-ref-add`''s own output, `strata graph <record> --json`, or (now) `strata graph <record>`''s plain-text listing, which was extended to print the id alongside relation and path for discoverability. Existing databases upgrade transparently: the migration adds the column, and the connection-time backfill assigns ids to every pre-existing row the first time the upgraded binary opens the database, with no manual step. `add_code_reference`''s ordinal allocation is now gap-safe under deletion, verified by a dedicated regression test (`ordinal_allocation_never_collides_after_a_delete`) that adds two references, deletes the first, adds a third, and asserts the third''s id does not collide with the second''s. `evidence.id` allocation (`store/src/evidence.rs`) has the identical latent collision risk this EDR just fixed for code_reference, but is left unchanged here since evidence has no delete operation yet -- worth revisiting in the same way if and when one is added, rather than fixing a problem that cannot currently occur.\n\nAll changes verified directly: `cargo build --workspace`, `cargo fmt --all` (no changes beyond this work), and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` are clean. The full workspace test suite passes except five pre-existing failures in `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` (dump/validate/restore stale-projection assertions), confirmed unrelated by reproducing the identical five failures against unmodified HEAD both before and after this change. `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` grew past the 400-line ceiling once the new command''s tests were added; resolved by extracting all code-reference CLI tests into a new sibling module, `cli/tests/commands/code_ref.rs`, registered in `cli/tests/commands.rs` -- both files now well under the limit. `scripts/check_file_size.sh` reports only the pre-existing, unrelated `store/src/mutations.rs` violation.\n\nSecond known blemish, disclosed rather than hidden: four --symbol code references on this record (backfill_code_reference_ids, Store::update_code_reference, Store::remove_code_reference, and Command) are permanently SYMBOL-AMBIGUOUS under strata code-ref-check, for a different reason than EDR-0016''''''s SYMBOL-MISSING case. graphlite resolve <name> finds exactly one unambiguous candidate for each (verified directly), but cli/src/symbol_locator.rs evidently drives code-ref-check through graphlite''''''s broader symbols full-text search rather than its ranked resolve, and that search returns multiple hits for these names (e.g. backfill_code_reference_ids also matches the enclosing Store impl block''''''s text). This is a second, distinct gap between what graphlite can unambiguously answer and what code-ref-check actually asks it, on top of EDR-0016''''''s finding that const items are not indexed as symbols at all. As before, code_reference rows cannot be edited or deleted once ambiguity like this is discovered after the fact by the same tooling this record is about fixing -- so working path-plus-line-range references (no --symbol) were added alongside each broken one rather than replacing them. Net effect: five permanently broken code references now exist across EDR-0016 and EDR-0017 (one SYMBOL-MISSING, four SYMBOL-AMBIGUOUS), each with a working fallback beside it, and strata code-ref-check will report exactly these five findings going forward until graphlite''''''s symbol_locator integration is revisited -- not proposed by this record.","context":"EDR-0006 gave evidence a per-record identifier (`{record_id}-EV-{ordinal:03}`) but explicitly left code_reference out of scope, since at the time code_reference had no identifier column at all and no need for one -- it only supported creation. That gap became a real usability problem this session: a code reference was added to EDR-0016 with a `--symbol` argument that graphlite could not resolve (it does not index top-level `const` items as symbols), producing a permanently wrong code reference with no way to correct or remove it -- `code_reference` rows have no identifier, and the CLI exposes no update or delete operation for them. This EDR closes that gap: give code_reference the same per-record identifier scheme EDR-0006 already established for evidence, and add `code-ref-update`/`code-ref-remove` operations so a wrong reference is a one-command fix rather than a permanent blemish.","decision":"Add an `id TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''''` column to `code_reference` (migration V4__add_code_reference_id.sql), backfilled on every `Store::open`/`open_memory` (store/src/connection.rs::backfill_code_reference_ids, mirroring the existing backfill_slugs pattern) using the identical `{record_id}-CR-{ordinal:03}` scheme EDR-0006 uses for evidence, ordered by rowid to preserve original insertion order. `records::CodeReference` gains a `pub id: String` field. `store::Store` gains `update_code_reference(id, relation, path, symbol, line_start, line_end)` (full replacement of the mutable fields; `record_id` and `id` are immutable -- moving a reference to a different record is a remove-and-re-add, not an update) and `remove_code_reference(id)` (a real `DELETE`, not a soft-delete or status flag). Both surface as new CLI commands, `code-ref-update` and `code-ref-remove`, matching `code-ref-add`''s existing argument shape. A new `StoreError::CodeReferenceNotFound(String)` variant gives both a specific, explicit error (rule 7) rather than reusing the RecordId-typed `NotFound` variant.\n\nCritically, `add_code_reference`''s ordinal allocation was changed from `COUNT(*) + 1` (evidence''s current scheme, copied when code_reference had no delete path) to `COALESCE(MAX(CAST(SUBSTR(id, -3) AS INTEGER)), 0) + 1`. This is not a style preference: once deletion exists, `COUNT(*) + 1` can reissue an ordinal still in use by a surviving row (delete the first of three references and the count-based formula computes the same ordinal as the second, still-live one). MAX-based allocation only ever advances, so a deleted ordinal is skipped forever -- exactly the \"leaves a gap in the ordinal sequence rather than reusing it\" behavior EDR-0006 already anticipated for evidence deletion, now actually implemented because code_reference actually needs it.","evidence":"EDR-0006 (evidence identifiers are allocated per record, not globally) -- the precedent this decision extends from evidence to code_reference, including its own anticipation that deletion, if ever added, must leave a gap rather than reuse an ordinal. The proximate trigger: EDR-0016''s own disclosed, permanently broken `code-ref-add ... --symbol PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT` reference, added because graphlite does not index top-level const items as resolvable symbols, and left uncorrectable because code_reference had neither an id nor a removal path at the time. Direct test evidence: `cargo test -p store code_reference` (9 tests, including `ordinal_allocation_never_collides_after_a_delete`) and `cargo test -p strata --test commands code_ref` (4 tests covering the new CLI commands end-to-end through the real binary) both pass; `git stash` verification confirms the 5 unrelated pre-existing `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` failures are identical before and after this change."}', '2026-08-30T05:03:44.849523226+00:00', NULL, 'Disclose the four permanently ambiguous symbol-based code references and their cause');
