he count-based formula computes the same ordinal as the second, still-live one). MAX-based allocation only ever advances, so a deleted ordinal is skipped forever -- exactly the \"leaves a gap in the ordinal sequence rather than reusing it\" behavior EDR-0006 already anticipated for evidence deletion, now actually implemented because code_reference actually needs it.","evidence":"EDR-0006 (evidence identifiers are allocated per record, not globally) -- the precedent this decision extends from evidence to code_reference, including its own anticipation that deletion, if ever added, must leave a gap rather than reuse an ordinal. The proximate trigger: EDR-0016''s own disclosed, permanently broken `code-ref-add ... --symbol PUBLISH_RENDERER_DEFAULT` reference, added because graphlite does not index top-level const items as resolvable symbols, and left uncorrectable because code_reference had neither an id nor a removal path at the time. Direct test evidence: `cargo test -p store code_reference` (9 tests, including `ordinal_allocation_never_collides_after_a_delete`) and `cargo test -p strata --test commands code_ref` (4 tests covering the new CLI commands end-to-end through the real binary) both pass; `git stash` verification confirms the 5 unrelated pre-existing `cli/tests/commands/auxiliary.rs` failures are identical before and after this change."}', '2026-08-30T05:03:44.876659349+00:00', NULL, 'status changed to proposed');
