bserved: a standalone decision, a deliberately retroactive record, and a decision expected to result from an RFC or PDR all look the same.\n\nThe lifecycle asymmetry is no longer open. EDR-0007 added Draft to ADR and EDR, matching RFC and PDR, while deliberately leaving search, validation, and export status-neutral. RFC-0003''s configuration boundary is also settled by ADR-0014 and EDR-0008; any future traceability strictness setting would consume that mechanism rather than redefine it here.","proposal":"Treat workflow policy as a set of explicit, inspectable conventions rather than as a mandatory pipeline:\n\n1. Use EDR-0007''s Draft status consistently across all four kinds. That decision is complete: Draft is discoverable, searchable, validated, and exported exactly like other statuses, and ADR/EDR must pass through Proposed before acceptance.\n\n2. Use ADR-0015''s advisory traceability policy. Accepted ADR/EDR records without incoming produces or derived-from lineage from an RFC/PDR receive a warning; the warning does not block lifecycle changes, and standalone or retroactive records remain valid.\n\n3. Use ADR-0016''s positive taxonomy test. ADR covers system-wide ownership, architectural boundaries, canonical state, and policy or contracts multiple components must honor. EDR covers local algorithms, formats, parameters, and implementation mechanisms within established boundaries. If both apply, classify the decision as ADR.","questions_for_review":"Published-versus-Committed remains deferred to a separate RFC unless future workflow evidence shows that the distinction is necessary to explain lifecycle state.","scope":"This RFC defines policy for a checkable but advisory form of cross-kind traceability, the interaction between that signal and retroactive records, and the classification boundary between ADR and EDR.\n\nEDR-0007, ADR-0015, and ADR-0016 resolve those workflow questions. RFC-0003, ADR-0014, and EDR-0008 resolve the configuration boundary. Published-versus-Committed remains outside this RFC and should receive its own proposal if it proves valuable."}', '2026-08-19T22:16:16.936264655+00:00', NULL, 'Align accepted RFC-0004 proposal with ADR-0016');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 1, '{"alternatives":"Publish engineering documentation and Rust documentation as separate sites. This minimizes integration work but loses the single navigable knowledge surface and encourages broken or duplicated cross-links.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc. This creates one apparent renderer pipeline, but rustdoc HTML contains generator-specific navigation, scripts, search metadata, and CSS behavior that Pandoc is not designed to preserve transparently.\n\nHard-code final relative URLs in Rust comments and engineering records. This is simple for a first site but couples source content to persisted slugs, directory layout, and future hosting changes.\n\nUse only ordinary Markdown links and manually maintain them. This avoids a custom resolver but recreates the link drift and stale-reference problem that canonical record identity and generated projections are intended to prevent.\n\nAdopt a full documentation framework such as mdBook or a static-site generator as the sole publisher. This may offer navigation and themes, but it does not remove the need to integrate rustdoc existing HTML structure and to define stable cross-reference ownership.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store and Markdown remains a generated projection. Generated record paths and persisted slugs must continue to be authoritative for Strata exports. Rustdoc intra-document links must continue to resolve through rustdoc rather than being replaced by a second semantic Rust symbol resolver. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static hosting, and able to fail when a declared cross-reference cannot be resolved. Stable source-level references should not depend on incidental output filenames. The workflow must preserve rustdoc HTML behavior and Pandoc document semantics as far as practical, and must not introduce async, server-side, or multi-user infrastructure merely for publication.","motivation":"The project plans to publish generated engineering documentation through a Pandoc-based workflow. Rustdoc separately produces standalone HTML documentation for the Rust crates. These outputs describe the same engineering system from complementary perspectives: records explain why choices were made, while Rust documentation explains the code that implements them. Publishing them as unrelated sites would make it harder to navigate from an engineering decision to the relevant API or from an API to the decision that constrains it. The discussion indicates that one static site containing both outputs is feasible if the publication workflow treats Pandoc and rustdoc as cooperating publishers.","non_goals":"This RFC does not select Pandoc as Strata canonical Markdown renderer, implement a publication command, change rustdoc, require a JavaScript runtime, or prescribe a web server. It does not require converting rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. It does not decide whether the published site should use a custom theme, mdBook, a static-site generator, or a particular hosting provider. It does not make the draft RFC or any cited engineering record an accepted decision.","outcome":"Draft. This RFC records the feasibility discussion and proposes a unified static site assembled from Pandoc-generated engineering documentation and rustdoc-generated Rust documentation. It does not accept the proposal or authorize implementation. Follow-on decisions should settle the reference syntax, manifest, link-resolution stage, publication command boundary, and treatment of rustdoc HTML before implementation begins.","problem":"There is no yet-defined publication contract for linking Strata engineering records and Rust documentation. Raw final URLs would couple source comments and records to generated filenames and site layout. Conversely, passing completed rustdoc HTML through Pandoc could damage rustdoc-specific markup, scripts, search behavior, CSS assumptions, and navigation. The project needs a design that preserves each generator strength while providing one stable site and one cross-reference model.","proposal":"Build the site as two generated document families followed by a deterministic assembly and validation phase. First, run strata export to produce canonical engineering Markdown and run Pandoc to produce engineering HTML. Second, run cargo doc to produce rustdoc HTML in a dedicated subtree. Third, generate a publication manifest from canonical Strata record identity and published paths, assemble a shared index and navigation links, and validate or rewrite cross-references using stable identifiers. Rust documentation should use an explicit stable reference convention such as a strata record identifier, while engineering documents should use the corresponding stable Rust documentation URL or a resolver-supported symbolic form. The exact syntax and rewrite mechanism should be settled by follow-on design records. The initial implementation should copy rustdoc output into the site rather than pass its completed HTML through Pandoc; Pandoc may still be used for engineering pages and for explicitly authored site-level pages.","questions_for_review":"1. Should publication be implemented as a new strata publish command, an external repository script, or a separate build tool that consumes strata export and cargo doc outputs?\n2. What stable reference syntax should source comments and engineering records use for links to records and Rust items?\n3. Should link resolution happen before generation, through Pandoc filters and rustdoc-compatible source links, or in a final assembly pass using a manifest?\n4. How should Rust API links be represented in engineering Markdown without making the Markdown projection depend on one publication root?\n5. What subset of rustdoc HTML may be themed or wrapped without breaking its generated navigation, search, source links, and static assets?\n6. Should the publication manifest include record revision, build identity, Rust toolchain identity, or only stable URLs and target identities?\n7. What should constitute a publication failure: unresolved record references, unresolved Rust references, missing generated assets, duplicate identifiers, or all of these?\n8. Should the first site publish only Strata crates and records, or support a separately configured project whose engineering records cite external Rust crates?","schema":"rfc/v1","scope":"Define a future static publication workflow that combines Pandoc-generated engineering-document pages with cargo doc or rustdoc-generated Rust API pages under one site root. The workflow includes a shared publication manifest mapping stable record identities such as RFC-0001 or ADR-0006 to generated URLs, cross-reference validation, site navigation, and a common landing page or shell. It covers references written in Rust documentation comments and engineering records, but not the content model of RFC, PDR, ADR, or EDR records."}', '2026-08-20T02:47:19.553142274+00:00', NULL, 'created');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 2, '{"alternatives":"Publish engineering documentation and Rustdoc as separate sites. This minimizes\nintegration work but preserves the broken-link and duplicated-navigation problem\nthat motivates this RFC.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc. This creates one apparent renderer\npipeline, but completed Rustdoc HTML contains generator-specific navigation,\nscripts, search metadata, and CSS behavior that Pandoc is not designed to preserve\ntransparently.\n\nHard-code final relative URLs in records and Rust documentation. This is simple\ninitially but couples source content to persisted slugs, directory layout, and\nhosting changes.\n\nAdopt mdBook or another static-site generator as the sole publisher. This may\nprovide themes and navigation, but it does not remove the need to preserve\nRustdoc HTML or define ownership of stable cross-references and generated paths.\n\nBuild the complete unified site in one step. This gives the desired end state\nquickly, but couples the first implementation to unresolved manifest, reference,\nasset, and failure semantics, making partial output difficult to validate.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store and Markdown remains a generated\nprojection. Persisted record slugs and canonical identifiers are authoritative;\npublication paths must be derived from them rather than from titles at publish\ntime. Rustdoc output must remain intact and must continue to own Rust intra-item\nlinks. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static\nhosting, and explicit about unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate\nidentities, or conflicting output paths. The design must remain compatible with\nthe specification''s single-process local workflow and must not add async,\nserver-side, or multi-user infrastructure.","motivation":"Engineering records explain why a system is shaped the way it is, while Rustdoc\nexplains the APIs and implementation that realize those choices. Today they are\npublished as unrelated outputs: Strata exports Markdown records, while Cargo\nproduces Rustdoc HTML. Readers therefore have no stable, generated path from a\ndecision such as ADR-0006 to the code it constrains, or from a Rust API back to\nthe decision that explains it.\n\nThe publication design should preserve each producer''s strengths. Strata owns\nrecord identity, relationships, metadata, and deterministic projections. Rustdoc\nowns Rust item links, navigation, search, and generated assets. Pandoc owns\ncompilation of engineering documents into formats such as HTML, PDF, and DOCX.","non_goals":"This RFC does not make Markdown or HTML canonical, replace Rustdoc, or pass\ncompleted Rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. It does not require a JavaScript runtime,\nserver, web application framework, or hosting provider. It does not define a\ntheme, a complete navigation design, or first-class publication-definition\nrecords. It does not require cross-references to be added to existing records or\nRust documentation before the first Strata-only package works.","outcome":"Draft, revised to define a staged publication contract rather than authorize the\nfull unified site. The next decision should settle the first package command\nboundary, manifest fields, and failure semantics. Implementation should begin\nwith deterministic Strata-only package generation and validation; Rustdoc\nassembly and broader site design remain follow-on work.","problem":"There is no publication contract that joins those outputs without coupling source\ncontent to incidental output filenames. Passing completed Rustdoc HTML through\nPandoc could damage Rustdoc''s scripts, CSS, navigation, and search behavior.\nConversely, manually writing final URLs into records or Rust documentation would\nmake links fragile when slugs, hosting roots, or generated layouts change.\n\nThe full static-site proposal is also too broad to implement as one step. It\ncombines record selection, metadata and URL ownership, cross-reference syntax,\nPandoc input, Rustdoc integration, site navigation, asset copying, and failure\nreporting. Those concerns need an incremental boundary that produces a useful,\ndeterministic artifact before Rustdoc integration is attempted.","proposal":"Build publication in two independently verifiable stages.\n\nFirst, produce a Strata publication package from canonical SQLite state. The\npackage contains a manifest mapping each selected record identity to its stable\npublication path, the generated Pandoc-compatible record input, and any metadata\nneeded by the site index. Record selection is derived from explicit query inputs\nsuch as kind, status, tag, or graph root; the default package may include all\nrecords. The package must be reproducible from the same database state and\nselection, and must fail before writing output when identities or paths collide.\n\nSecond, assemble the package with Rustdoc output copied into a dedicated subtree,\nwithout rewriting completed Rustdoc HTML. The assembler owns the shared site\nroot, landing page, record navigation, search-index inputs, asset layout, and\ncross-reference validation. Pandoc compiles only the engineering inputs and\nexplicit site pages. Rustdoc remains the publisher of Rust API pages.\n\nStable cross-references use identity-based declarations rather than final URLs.\nThe first contract should support record identifiers such as `ADR-0006` in\nengineering publication inputs and a separately defined Rust item reference form\nin Rust documentation. The manifest and final site root resolve those declarations\nto URLs during assembly. A publication fails on an unresolved declaration; it\ndoes not silently preserve a broken or guessed URL.\n\nThe first implementation should therefore stop after the deterministic Strata\npublication package and its validation. Rustdoc copying, site assembly, and\ncross-reference rewriting follow only after the package format and path ownership\nare tested against real Strata records.","questions_for_review":"1. Should the first Strata publication package be produced by a new `strata\n   publish` command, an external repository build script, or a small separate\n   tool that consumes `strata export` and the database?\n2. What exact syntax should Rust documentation use for a stable reference to a\n   Strata record or to a publication-owned Rust item?\n3. Should the manifest include record revision, Strata build identity, Rust\n   toolchain identity, and source commit, or only identities and paths?\n4. Which graph queries and selection filters are required for the first package?\n5. Which unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate identities, and output\n   collisions are fatal in each publication stage?","scope":"This RFC defines the staged publication contract for Strata''s own workspace:\n\n- a deterministic publication manifest derived from canonical record identity,\n  persisted slugs, status, revision, and generated record paths;\n- a Strata-only publication package containing selected records and\n  Pandoc-compatible Markdown or equivalent input;\n- a later assembly stage that places the Strata package beside unmodified\n  Rustdoc output under one static-site root; and\n- validation of generated paths, required assets, duplicate identities, and\n  declared cross-references before publication succeeds.\n\nThe first implementation targets Strata''s own records and crates. Supporting a\nseparately configured project is a later extension once the local contract has\nbeen exercised."}', '2026-08-20T03:42:03.237178660+00:00', NULL, 'narrow publication RFC into staged package and assembly phases');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 3, '{"alternatives":"Publish engineering documentation and Rustdoc as separate sites. This minimizes\nintegration work but preserves the broken-link and duplicated-navigation problem\nthat motivates this RFC.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc. This creates one apparent renderer\npipeline, but completed Rustdoc HTML contains generator-specific navigation,\nscripts, search metadata, and CSS behavior that Pandoc is not designed to preserve\ntransparently.\n\nHard-code final relative URLs in records and Rust documentation. This is simple\ninitially but couples source content to persisted slugs, directory layout, and\nhosting changes.\n\nAdopt mdBook or another static-site generator as the sole publisher. This may\nprovide themes and navigation, but it does not remove the need to preserve\nRustdoc HTML or define ownership of stable cross-references and generated paths.\n\nBuild the complete unified site in one step. This gives the desired end state\nquickly, but couples the first implementation to unresolved manifest, reference,\nasset, and failure semantics, making partial output difficult to validate.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store and Markdown remains a generated\nprojection. Persisted record slugs and canonical identifiers are authoritative;\npublication paths must be derived from them rather than from titles at publish\ntime. Rustdoc output must remain intact and must continue to own Rust intra-item\nlinks. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static\nhosting, and explicit about unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate\nidentities, or conflicting output paths. The design must remain compatible with\nthe specification''s single-process local workflow and must not add async,\nserver-side, or multi-user infrastructure.","motivation":"Engineering records explain why a system is shaped the way it is, while Rustdoc\nexplains the APIs and implementation that realize those choices. Today they are\npublished as unrelated outputs: Strata exports Markdown records, while Cargo\nproduces Rustdoc HTML. Readers therefore have no stable, generated path from a\ndecision such as ADR-0006 to the code it constrains, or from a Rust API back to\nthe decision that explains it.\n\nThe publication design should preserve each producer''s strengths. Strata owns\nrecord identity, relationships, metadata, and deterministic projections. Rustdoc\nowns Rust item links, navigation, search, and generated assets. Pandoc owns\ncompilation of engineering documents into formats such as HTML, PDF, and DOCX.","non_goals":"This RFC does not make Markdown or HTML canonical, replace Rustdoc, or pass\ncompleted Rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. It does not require a JavaScript runtime,\nserver, web application framework, or hosting provider. It does not define a\ntheme, a complete navigation design, or first-class publication-definition\nrecords. It does not require cross-references to be added to existing records or\nRust documentation before the first Strata-only package works.","outcome":"Draft, revised to define a staged publication contract rather than authorize the\nfull unified site. The next decision should settle the first package command\nboundary, manifest fields, and failure semantics. Implementation should begin\nwith deterministic Strata-only package generation and validation; Rustdoc\nassembly and broader site design remain follow-on work.","problem":"There is no publication contract that joins those outputs without coupling source\ncontent to incidental output filenames. Passing completed Rustdoc HTML through\nPandoc could damage Rustdoc''s scripts, CSS, navigation, and search behavior.\nConversely, manually writing final URLs into records or Rust documentation would\nmake links fragile when slugs, hosting roots, or generated layouts change.\n\nThe full static-site proposal is also too broad to implement as one step. It\ncombines record selection, metadata and URL ownership, cross-reference syntax,\nPandoc input, Rustdoc integration, site navigation, asset copying, and failure\nreporting. Those concerns need an incremental boundary that produces a useful,\ndeterministic artifact before Rustdoc integration is attempted.","proposal":"Build publication in two independently verifiable stages.\n\nFirst, produce a Strata publication package from canonical SQLite state. The\npackage contains a manifest mapping each selected record identity to its stable\npublication path, the generated Pandoc-compatible record input, and any metadata\nneeded by the site index. Record selection is derived from explicit query inputs\nsuch as kind, status, tag, or graph root; the default package may include all\nrecords. The package must be reproducible from the same database state and\nselection, and must fail before writing output when identities or paths collide.\n\nSecond, assemble the package with Rustdoc output copied into a dedicated subtree,\nwithout rewriting completed Rustdoc HTML. The assembler owns the shared site\nroot, landing page, record navigation, search-index inputs, asset layout, and\ncross-reference validation. Pandoc compiles only the engineering inputs and\nexplicit site pages. Rustdoc remains the publisher of Rust API pages.\n\nStable cross-references use identity-based declarations rather than final URLs.\nThe first contract should support record identifiers such as `ADR-0006` in\nengineering publication inputs and a separately defined Rust item reference form\nin Rust documentation. The manifest and final site root resolve those declarations\nto URLs during assembly. A publication fails on an unresolved declaration; it\ndoes not silently preserve a broken or guessed URL.\n\nThe first implementation should therefore stop after the deterministic Strata\npublication package and its validation. Rustdoc copying, site assembly, and\ncross-reference rewriting follow only after the package format and path ownership\nare tested against real Strata records.","questions_for_review":"1. Should the first Strata publication package be produced by a new `strata\n   publish` command, an external repository build script, or a small separate\n   tool that consumes `strata export` and the database?\n2. What exact syntax should Rust documentation use for a stable reference to a\n   Strata record or to a publication-owned Rust item?\n3. Should the manifest include record revision, Strata build identity, Rust\n   toolchain identity, and source commit, or only identities and paths?\n4. Which graph queries and selection filters are required for the first package?\n5. Which unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate identities, and output\n   collisions are fatal in each publication stage?","scope":"This RFC defines the staged publication contract for Strata''s own workspace:\n\n- a deterministic publication manifest derived from canonical record identity,\n  persisted slugs, status, revision, and generated record paths;\n- a Strata-only publication package containing selected records and\n  Pandoc-compatible Markdown or equivalent input;\n- a later assembly stage that places the Strata package beside unmodified\n  Rustdoc output under one static-site root; and\n- validation of generated paths, required assets, duplicate identities, and\n  declared cross-references before publication succeeds.\n\nThe first implementation targets Strata''s own records and crates. Supporting a\nseparately configured project is a later extension once the local contract has\nbeen exercised."}', '2026-08-20T06:15:00.161703402+00:00', NULL, 'title changed to ''Publish Strata''s canonical knowledge as a static site''');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 4, '{"alternatives":"Continue publishing only generated Markdown. This preserves portability but does not provide the navigable, reviewable static artifact needed for local use.\n\nUse mdBook or another general static-site generator as the information-architecture owner. This provides an existing renderer and theme, but does not model Strata''s record graph or remove the need for Strata-generated navigation and traceability views.\n\nUse a Git forge''s Pages feature as the publication system. This couples local review to a hosting provider and makes private publication dependent on provider-specific behavior. Git remains a useful review and distribution surface, but static hosting should work without a forge.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc, including Rustdoc HTML. This risks damaging Rustdoc''s generator-specific navigation, scripts, CSS, and search behavior. Rustdoc integration may be added later as a separate assembly stage that preserves Rustdoc output intact.\n\nEdit generated Markdown or HTML directly after publication. This creates a second write path and contradicts the canonical SQLite model established by ADR-0001 and ADR-0003.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store, as established by ADR-0001. Markdown and all Pandoc-derived output remain generated projections, as established by ADR-0003. Strata owns site information architecture and graph-derived navigation, as established by ADR-0004. Persisted record slugs and canonical identifiers are authoritative; publication paths must be derived from them rather than from titles at publish time. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static hosting, and explicit about unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate identities, or conflicting output paths. A failed publication must not leave a partially replaced site. The design must remain compatible with Strata''s single-process local workflow and must not add async, server-side, or multi-user infrastructure.","motivation":"Strata''s canonical knowledge is useful only if people and tools can review it without opening the SQLite database. The publication layer should turn the current database state into a deterministic, navigable static site for local review and later distribution. Strata owns the engineering knowledge model, while Pandoc and an ordinary static web server provide portable presentation and hosting.","non_goals":"This RFC does not make Markdown or HTML canonical, replace SQLite, define a Git forge, or require GitHub, GitLab, Forgejo, or Gitea. It does not require a web application, JavaScript runtime, server-side rendering, or a multi-user publication service. It does not make Rustdoc part of the first publication artifact and does not pass completed Rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. Rustdoc assembly and cross-links between Rust API pages and Strata records remain optional follow-on work.","outcome":"Draft, revised to define SQLite-to-static-site publication as the core of RFC-0005. Strata owns canonical data, projections, site information architecture, and publication validation; Pandoc renders documents; Apache or another static server hosts the resulting artifact. Git hosting is optional for review, backup, mirroring, and distribution. Rustdoc integration is deferred and must not shape the first implementation boundary. The next design record should settle the publication command boundary, manifest, output layout, staging and replacement semantics, and initial failure policy.","problem":"SQLite is the canonical store, but database records are not a convenient publication format. Markdown projections are suitable for review and interchange, while HTML is suitable for browsing. A publication contract is needed to define how Strata derives both from canonical state, how navigation follows the record graph, and how generated output can be safely replaced without introducing a second source of truth. This contract should remain independent of Git hosting providers and should not require a web application or multi-user service.","proposal":"Build static publication as a deterministic pipeline from canonical state.\n\nFirst, Strata validates the selected database state and generates Markdown projections plus a publication manifest. The manifest maps each selected record identity to its stable publication path and includes the metadata needed for indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification hierarchy, and traceability reports. Selection may initially default to all records and later support filters such as kind, status, tag, or graph root.\n\nSecond, Strata or a publication script passes the generated engineering inputs and explicit site pages to Pandoc. Pandoc renders individual documents using the selected template, metadata, and stylesheet. Strata—not Pandoc—owns page selection, navigation, indexes, and graph-derived views.\n\nThird, publication writes the complete static artifact to a staging directory and replaces the configured output directory only after generation and validation succeed. The artifact contains HTML, CSS, images, and any other required assets and can be served directly by Apache on the local S7 or by another ordinary static server. Hosting is deliberately outside the Strata domain model.\n\nA normal record mutation may be followed by export and publication so the site reflects current database state. The first interface should provide explicit commands with deterministic results; a watch mode or automatic local regeneration can be added later without changing the canonical model. Generated Markdown and HTML may be committed or mirrored for review, but they never override SQLite.\n\nThe publication manifest and generated site should identify the database revision, record revisions, Strata build identity, and publication inputs used to produce them. A publication fails on unresolved required references, duplicate identities, output collisions, missing required assets, or invalid record state; it does not silently publish incomplete output.","questions_for_review":"1. Should `strata publish` initially combine Markdown export, Pandoc invocation, site assembly, and validation, or should Pandoc orchestration remain an external repository script?\n2. Which publication filters are required for the first useful site: record kind, status, tag, specification subtree, or graph root?\n3. Which manifest fields are required beyond identity, path, status, and revision: database revision, Strata build identity, source commit, or template identity?\n4. Should the first local publisher write directly to a configured Apache document root, or always produce an artifact that a separate deployment step installs?\n5. Which references are required to resolve before publication, and which may be reported as warnings?\n6. Should public and internal publication profiles be represented in Strata, or remain deployment-level selection of records and fields?","scope":"This RFC defines the first static publication contract for Strata:\n\n- SQLite remains the sole canonical source of record content, identity, status, revisions, and relationships.\n- Strata generates deterministic Markdown projections and publication metadata from that state.\n- Pandoc compiles engineering documents and site pages into static HTML using Strata-owned templates and styles.\n- Strata generates indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification views, and traceability reports from the record graph.\n- The result is a self-contained static artifact suitable for Apache or another ordinary static web server.\n\nThe first implementation targets Strata''s own records and a local publication directory. Supporting separately configured projects, public/private publication profiles, and additional output formats may follow once this contract is exercised."}', '2026-08-20T06:15:11.553823969+00:00', NULL, 'Narrow publication to the canonical SQLite-to-static-site pipeline');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 5, '{"alternatives":"Continue publishing only generated Markdown. This preserves portability but does not provide the navigable, reviewable static artifact needed for local use.\n\nUse mdBook or another general static-site generator as the information-architecture owner. This provides an existing renderer and theme, but does not model Strata''s record graph or remove the need for Strata-generated navigation and traceability views.\n\nUse a Git forge''s Pages feature as the publication system. This couples local review to a hosting provider and makes private publication dependent on provider-specific behavior. Git remains a useful review and distribution surface, but static hosting should work without a forge.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc, including Rustdoc HTML. This risks damaging Rustdoc''s generator-specific navigation, scripts, CSS, and search behavior. Rustdoc integration may be added later as a separate assembly stage that preserves Rustdoc output intact.\n\nEdit generated Markdown or HTML directly after publication. This creates a second write path and contradicts the canonical SQLite model established by ADR-0001 and ADR-0003.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store, as established by ADR-0001. Markdown and all Pandoc-derived output remain generated projections, as established by ADR-0003. Strata owns site information architecture and graph-derived navigation, as established by ADR-0004. Persisted record slugs and canonical identifiers are authoritative; publication paths must be derived from them rather than from titles at publish time. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static hosting, and explicit about unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate identities, or conflicting output paths. A failed publication must not leave a partially replaced site. The design must remain compatible with Strata''s single-process local workflow and must not add async, server-side, or multi-user infrastructure.","motivation":"Strata''s canonical knowledge is useful only if people and tools can review it without opening the SQLite database. The publication layer should turn the current database state into a deterministic, navigable static site for local review and later distribution. Strata owns the engineering knowledge model, while Pandoc and an ordinary static web server provide portable presentation and hosting.","non_goals":"This RFC does not make Markdown or HTML canonical, replace SQLite, define a Git forge, or require GitHub, GitLab, Forgejo, or Gitea. It does not require a web application, JavaScript runtime, server-side rendering, or a multi-user publication service. It does not make Rustdoc part of the first publication artifact and does not pass completed Rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. Rustdoc assembly and cross-links between Rust API pages and Strata records remain optional follow-on work.","outcome":"Draft, revised to define SQLite-to-static-site publication as the core of RFC-0005. Strata owns canonical data, projections, site information architecture, and publication validation; a configured static-site backend renders documents; an ordinary static server hosts the resulting artifact. Git hosting is optional for review, backup, mirroring, and distribution. Rustdoc integration is deferred and must not shape the first implementation boundary.\n\nPDR-0002 (static publication design) now settles the publication command boundary, backend configurability, output target, and staging/replacement semantics: `strata publish` is a single composition-root command (ADR-0017); it always produces a staged artifact directory and never writes to a live document root (ADR-0018); and its static-site backend is invoked as an externally configured subprocess command rather than a compiled Rust trait, so a different generator can be selected without a Strata code change (ADR-0019). This resolves Open Questions 1 and 4. Open Questions 2 and 6 (publication filters, public/internal profiles) remain deferred until the single-profile pipeline is exercised; Open Question 3 (manifest fields) has a first answer in PDR-0002 with exact types left to implementation; Open Question 5 (required references) is answered at baseline by reusing `strata validate`''s existing ERROR set. This RFC remains draft pending implementation and resolution of the remaining open questions.","problem":"SQLite is the canonical store, but database records are not a convenient publication format. Markdown projections are suitable for review and interchange, while HTML is suitable for browsing. A publication contract is needed to define how Strata derives both from canonical state, how navigation follows the record graph, and how generated output can be safely replaced without introducing a second source of truth. This contract should remain independent of Git hosting providers and should not require a web application or multi-user service.","proposal":"Build static publication as a deterministic pipeline from canonical state.\n\nFirst, Strata validates the selected database state and generates Markdown projections plus a publication manifest. The manifest maps each selected record identity to its stable publication path and includes the metadata needed for indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification hierarchy, and traceability reports. Selection may initially default to all records and later support filters such as kind, status, tag, or graph root.\n\nSecond, Strata or a publication script passes the generated engineering inputs and explicit site pages to Pandoc. Pandoc renders individual documents using the selected template, metadata, and stylesheet. Strata—not Pandoc—owns page selection, navigation, indexes, and graph-derived views.\n\nThird, publication writes the complete static artifact to a staging directory and replaces the configured output directory only after generation and validation succeed. The artifact contains HTML, CSS, images, and any other required assets and can be served directly by Apache on the local S7 or by another ordinary static server. Hosting is deliberately outside the Strata domain model.\n\nA normal record mutation may be followed by export and publication so the site reflects current database state. The first interface should provide explicit commands with deterministic results; a watch mode or automatic local regeneration can be added later without changing the canonical model. Generated Markdown and HTML may be committed or mirrored for review, but they never override SQLite.\n\nThe publication manifest and generated site should identify the database revision, record revisions, Strata build identity, and publication inputs used to produce them. A publication fails on unresolved required references, duplicate identities, output collisions, missing required assets, or invalid record state; it does not silently publish incomplete output.","questions_for_review":"1. Which publication filters are required for the first useful site: record kind, status, tag, specification subtree, or graph root? -- still open, deferred by PDR-0002 until the single-profile pipeline is exercised.\n2. Which manifest fields are required beyond identity, path, status, and revision? -- PDR-0002''s Data Model gives a first answer (schema version, generated_at, Strata build identity, database identity, renderer identity, per-entry content hash); exact JSON field types remain a follow-on EDR once `strata publish` is implemented.\n3. Which references are required to resolve before publication, and which may be reported as warnings? -- PDR-0002''s Failure Modes answers this at baseline: publish''s precondition is exactly `strata validate`''s existing ERROR set (including EDR-0011''s projection-staleness checks) plus path-collision and renderer-failure cases specific to rendering.\n4. Should public and internal publication profiles be represented in Strata, or remain deployment-level selection of records and fields? -- still open, deferred by PDR-0002 for the same reason as filters.","scope":"This RFC defines the first static publication contract for Strata:\n\n- SQLite remains the sole canonical source of record content, identity, status, revisions, and relationships.\n- Strata generates deterministic Markdown projections and publication metadata from that state.\n- Pandoc compiles engineering documents and site pages into static HTML using Strata-owned templates and styles.\n- Strata generates indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification views, and traceability reports from the record graph.\n- The result is a self-contained static artifact suitable for Apache or another ordinary static web server.\n\nThe first implementation targets Strata''s own records and a local publication directory. Supporting separately configured projects, public/private publication profiles, and additional output formats may follow once this contract is exercised."}', '2026-08-20T16:19:33.374452580+00:00', NULL, 'Reconcile against PDR-0002: resolve Open Questions 1 and 4, note baseline answers for 3 and 5, defer 2 and 6');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0005', 6, '{"alternatives":"Continue publishing only generated Markdown. This preserves portability but does not provide the navigable, reviewable static artifact needed for local use.\n\nUse mdBook or another general static-site generator as the information-architecture owner. This provides an existing renderer and theme, but does not model Strata''s record graph or remove the need for Strata-generated navigation and traceability views.\n\nUse a Git forge''s Pages feature as the publication system. This couples local review to a hosting provider and makes private publication dependent on provider-specific behavior. Git remains a useful review and distribution surface, but static hosting should work without a forge.\n\nRun all generated output through Pandoc, including Rustdoc HTML. This risks damaging Rustdoc''s generator-specific navigation, scripts, CSS, and search behavior. Rustdoc integration may be added later as a separate assembly stage that preserves Rustdoc output intact.\n\nEdit generated Markdown or HTML directly after publication. This creates a second write path and contradicts the canonical SQLite model established by ADR-0001 and ADR-0003.","constraints":"SQLite remains the canonical record store, as established by ADR-0001. Markdown and all Pandoc-derived output remain generated projections, as established by ADR-0003. Strata owns site information architecture and graph-derived navigation, as established by ADR-0004. Persisted record slugs and canonical identifiers are authoritative; publication paths must be derived from them rather than from titles at publish time. Publication must be deterministic, locally buildable, suitable for static hosting, and explicit about unresolved references, missing assets, duplicate identities, or conflicting output paths. A failed publication must not leave a partially replaced site. The design must remain compatible with Strata''s single-process local workflow and must not add async, server-side, or multi-user infrastructure.","motivation":"Strata''s canonical knowledge is useful only if people and tools can review it without opening the SQLite database. The publication layer should turn the current database state into a deterministic, navigable static site for local review and later distribution. Strata owns the engineering knowledge model, while Pandoc and an ordinary static web server provide portable presentation and hosting.","non_goals":"This RFC does not make Markdown or HTML canonical, replace SQLite, define a Git forge, or require GitHub, GitLab, Forgejo, or Gitea. It does not require a web application, JavaScript runtime, server-side rendering, or a multi-user publication service. It does not make Rustdoc part of the first publication artifact and does not pass completed Rustdoc HTML through Pandoc. Rustdoc assembly and cross-links between Rust API pages and Strata records remain optional follow-on work.","outcome":"Draft, revised to define SQLite-to-static-site publication as the core of RFC-0005. Strata owns canonical data, projections, site information architecture, and publication validation; a configured static-site backend renders documents; an ordinary static server hosts the resulting artifact. Git hosting is optional for review, backup, mirroring, and distribution. Rustdoc integration is deferred and must not shape the first implementation boundary.\n\nPDR-0002 (static publication design) now settles the publication command boundary, backend configurability, output target, and staging/replacement semantics: `strata publish` is a single composition-root command (ADR-0017); it always produces a staged artifact directory and never writes to a live document root (ADR-0018); and its static-site backend is invoked as an externally configured subprocess command rather than a compiled Rust trait, so a different generator can be selected without a Strata code change (ADR-0019). This resolves Open Questions 1 and 4. Open Questions 2 and 6 (publication filters, public/internal profiles) remain deferred until the single-profile pipeline is exercised; Open Question 3 (manifest fields) has a first answer in PDR-0002 with exact types left to implementation; Open Question 5 (required references) is answered at baseline by reusing `strata validate`''s existing ERROR set. This RFC remains draft pending implementation and resolution of the remaining open questions.","problem":"SQLite is the canonical store, but database records are not a convenient publication format. Markdown projections are suitable for review and interchange, while HTML is suitable for browsing. A publication contract is needed to define how Strata derives both from canonical state, how navigation follows the record graph, and how generated output can be safely replaced without introducing a second source of truth. This contract should remain independent of Git hosting providers and should not require a web application or multi-user service.","proposal":"Build static publication as a deterministic pipeline from canonical state.\n\nFirst, Strata validates the selected database state and generates Markdown projections plus a publication manifest. The manifest maps each selected record identity to its stable publication path and includes the metadata needed for indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification hierarchy, and traceability reports. Selection may initially default to all records and later support filters such as kind, status, tag, or graph root.\n\nSecond, Strata or a publication script passes the generated engineering inputs and explicit site pages to Pandoc. Pandoc renders individual documents using the selected template, metadata, and stylesheet. Strata—not Pandoc—owns page selection, navigation, indexes, and graph-derived views.\n\nThird, publication writes the complete static artifact to a staging directory and replaces the configured output directory only after generation and validation succeed. The artifact contains HTML, CSS, images, and any other required assets and can be served directly by Apache on the local S7 or by another ordinary static server. Hosting is deliberately outside the Strata domain model.\n\nA normal record mutation may be followed by export and publication so the site reflects current database state. The first interface should provide explicit commands with deterministic results; a watch mode or automatic local regeneration can be added later without changing the canonical model. Generated Markdown and HTML may be committed or mirrored for review, but they never override SQLite.\n\nThe publication manifest and generated site should identify the database revision, record revisions, Strata build identity, and publication inputs used to produce them. A publication fails on unresolved required references, duplicate identities, output collisions, missing required assets, or invalid record state; it does not silently publish incomplete output.","questions_for_review":"1. Which publication filters are required for the first useful site: record kind, status, tag, specification subtree, or graph root? -- still open, deferred by PDR-0002 until the single-profile pipeline is exercised.\n2. Which manifest fields are required beyond identity, path, status, and revision? -- PDR-0002''s Data Model gives a first answer (schema version, generated_at, Strata build identity, database identity, renderer identity, per-entry content hash); exact JSON field types remain a follow-on EDR once `strata publish` is implemented.\n3. Which references are required to resolve before publication, and which may be reported as warnings? -- PDR-0002''s Failure Modes answers this at baseline: publish''s precondition is exactly `strata validate`''s existing ERROR set (including EDR-0011''s projection-staleness checks) plus path-collision and renderer-failure cases specific to rendering.\n4. Should public and internal publication profiles be represented in Strata, or remain deployment-level selection of records and fields? -- still open, deferred by PDR-0002 for the same reason as filters.","scope":"This RFC defines the first static publication contract for Strata:\n\n- SQLite remains the sole canonical source of record content, identity, status, revisions, and relationships.\n- Strata generates deterministic Markdown projections and publication metadata from that state.\n- Pandoc compiles engineering documents and site pages into static HTML using Strata-owned templates and styles.\n- Strata generates indexes, navigation, relationship views, specification views, and traceability reports from the record graph.\n- The result is a self-contained static artifact suitable for Apache or another ordinary static web server.\n\nThe first implementation targets Strata''s own records and a local publication directory. Supporting separately configured projects, public/private publication profiles, and additional output formats may follow once this contract is exercised."}', '2026-08-20T16:36:55.775895076+00:00', NULL, 'status changed to proposed');
